{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~10 trading days (often +5 to +12 EUR/t by days 15–30). Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon.",
    "Regime-change blindness / delayed downturn recognition: when the recent history contains a local peak/spike (e.g., ~71–73), the model extrapolates strength, but the market mean-reverts and then sells off. Add a mean-reversion term that pulls forecasts toward the 30d trailing mean and strengthens after sharp up-moves.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following short-term rallies, the model predicts continued grind higher (72–74), while outcomes roll over quickly (down to low/mid-60s). Cap upside continuation: if last 5–10d momentum is strongly positive, dampen forecast slope and reduce levels for horizons >7d.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small; mid/long horizon errors widen materially. Use piecewise correction: minimal adjustment for days 1–5, moderate for days 6–10, strong downward + mean-reversion for days 11–30.",
    "Underestimation of downside volatility: realized paths show sharper drops than forecast. Introduce asymmetric adjustment: when recent volatility is elevated or price is above its 20–30d average, skew corrections downward more than upward.",
    "Trend persistence overestimated: model maintains a high plateau (around 71–73) even as realized prices transition to a lower range (mid-60s). Add a 'range-shift' heuristic: if price is > (30d mean + 1σ), pull forecasts back toward mean by an increasing fraction with horizon (e.g., 30% at 10d, 60% at 30d)."
  ]
}
